>[]($block.attrs("author-note"))
> It's only month 4 and I'm alread running out of clever ways to open these.

# Stuff I Did
## Ran
* I started my marathon training block in earnest this month, after moving my target race
to midway through October. I'm trying to start early this go around since I'm aiming to run
a 3:30 at that race. I have not been super consistent with it so far, but I have at least
not skipped a long run, which is the most important thing.
* Here's my output from [thoth](https://codeberg.org/garrettm/thoth), which now is useful
enough to give me a summary:
```
❯ thoth summary --since month
=== THIS MONTH ===
distance (km): 72.735
average speed (km/hr): 11.813
total_time (hr): 6.41
```

## Played
* I only played one full game this month, which was Hi-Fi RUSH. I wrote a
[Rambling Review](rr-hifi-rush) for it.
* I played a smattering of other stuff on and off, mostly stuff that I could pick up and
enjoy for just a few minutes at a time. [ULTRAKILL](https://store.steampowered.com/app/1229490/ULTRAKILL/)
was a notable one for being intense enough that I could only play in short bursts anyway.

## Read
### Books
* After falling back into the Red Rising universe (in a good way, because the books are
excellent) I powered through both [Dark Age](https://hardcover.app/books/dark-age) and
[Light Bringer](https://hardcover.app/books/light-bringer) this month. Just like with the
original trilogy, it only got stronger as it went along, and even though I could see some
of the major story beats coming it was still as gripping as I could have hoped. I can't
wait for *Red God* to come out.

### Not Books
As is becoming a trend (and one I want to reduce, more on that later) I read a ton of tech
articles, far too many about LLMs. A selection:

* [I used AI. It worked. I hated it.](https://taggart-tech.com/reckoning/) - rough one to
read, especially because it mirrors my own experience with the few instances I've tried in
good faith to use the tools. I still personally sit very much in the "not a notable
improvement and in many ways a detriment" camp, and will continue to refrain from using
LLMs in my work.
* [The Machines are Fine, I'm Worried About Us](https://ergosphere.blog/posts/the-machines-are-fine/)
(this seems to have been taken down?) either way, it has a salient quote that I thankfully
saved from when I first read it:
> Schwartz's experiment is the most revealing, and not for the reason he thinks. What he
> demonstrated is that Claude can, with detailed supervision, produce a technically
> rigorous physics paper. What he actually demonstrated, if you read carefully, is that
> the supervision is the physics. [...] The experiment succeeded because the human
> supervisor had done the grunt work, years ago, that the machine is now supposedly
> liberating us from.

* Like many in my industry, I read the entire [The Future of Everything is Lies, I Guess](https://aphyr.com/posts/411-the-future-of-everything-is-lies-i-guess)
(the introduction is linked). It is really, really depressing. But I think it's also
really really important reading. If you're going to sit down with a nice cup of *insert
preferred beverage* and read anything I've linked, it should be this one.
* I also read [Go Ahead And Use AI. It Will Only Help Me Dominate You.](https://defector.com/go-ahead-and-use-ai-it-will-only-help-me-dominate-you),
which maybe makes me a bit full of myself but was entertaining nonetheless.
* Phew, ok, enough with the LLM articles. How about some rapid-fire stuff that was just
plain interesting or funny rather than depressing:
    * [The Top 10 Biggest Conspiracies in Open Source](https://nesbitt.io/2026/03/25/the-top-10-biggest-conspiracies-in-open-source.html)
    was an outstanding slow build of absurdity.
    * [coreutils](https://ratfactor.com/slackware/pkgblog/coreutils), which goes through
    every single GNU core utility, was both fascinating and super useful especially since
    my new job has me spending a lot of time SSHed into a remote system with only a bash
    shell to my name.
    * [Drunk Post: Things I’ve Learned as a Senior Engineer](https://luminousmen.substack.com/p/drunk-post-things-ive-learned-as)
    was highly entertaining even though none of it is particularly novel.
    * Finally, [CHANGELOG.md](https://nesbitt.io/2026/02/16/changelog.html) was top-tier satire.

